{"connection_id": "BC-4b20e301834c12ee6d1b", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions 10.6.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "earth, sea, sky, sun, moon, and stars say they are not God", "note": "Augustine stages the commentary's distinction in first-person form: visible creatures direct the seeker beyond themselves to their maker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d070c9800e3915f8f9fc", "ref": "Didache 1.1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways of life and death", "note": "The opening Two Ways instruction gives a Christian communal formulation for the commentary's straight route and its opposed lost route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-797a231f8d8715ce54a3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses's generation drinks from the spiritual rock, identified as Christ", "note": "Paul explicitly rereads the wilderness water episode, extending the commentary's Moses-water meeting through a Christian interpretive claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9a2e3548091ae9281b9d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.8.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one God and one Lord through whom all things are", "note": "In the context of many so-called heavenly gods and lords, Paul names one God and one Lord, closely matching the commentary's rejection of celestial rivals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b3dbb05b926b79d85a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "free people, yet servants of God", "note": "The verse expressly holds freedom and God's service together, directly illuminating the commentary's freely chosen kul."}
{"connection_id": "BC-31bea8688a177feff826", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straying sheep returned to Shepherd and Overseer", "note": "First Peter explicitly turns straying into return to a shepherd, a direct Christian parallel to dallin and guided recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ef2cf6d074e4b7d209e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spiritual house, priesthood, and spiritual sacrifices", "note": "First Peter unites house and sacrifice in a worshipping community, providing a Christian counterpart to the commentary's destination-house and sacrificial herd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6849f1838a9ee5c2c064", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turn from idols to serve a living and true God", "note": "The conversion formula explicitly combines leaving false objects of service with serving the living true God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec52b410d2d522bc7667", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all appear before Christ's judgment seat to receive what is due", "note": "The verse treats final judgment as recompense owed, a precise Christian counterpart to din as a day of settled debt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bcb7cb5e87afe4bd926a", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forsook the straight way and went astray", "note": "Second Peter uses the paired language of a straight way and straying, directly matching the commentary's upright walker and dallin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d48b71e8d67add16ee5", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lord knows those who are his; the foundation bears a seal", "note": "The seal combines known ownership and moral departure from evil, directly reversing the commentary's animal with no known master."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2b68eda379d81b3d835", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.14.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain, fruitful seasons, food from heaven", "note": "Paul's speech identifies the Creator through rain, crops, and food, a close Christian counterpart to the commentary's clouds and plants nourished by the Rabb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f5070e78ed0e1cb0ab1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "creator is Lord of heaven and earth", "note": "Paul names the maker of the world and all it contains as Lord of heaven and earth, a direct Christian counterpart to Rabb al-alamin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5291c43111d033084832", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives life and breath to all", "note": "Paul says God gives everyone life, breath, and everything, matching the commentary's Lord of all worlds who gives nourishment and help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-557f2723625933060f1b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.26", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God appoints times and boundaries", "note": "Paul says God made all nations and set their times and boundaries, linking universal lordship to the ordered movement of peoples."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b2ff264b7bc66f7182d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.27", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People seek God and may find him", "note": "The nations are placed so they might seek God, a direct meeting with the commentary's lost seeker, guidance, and discovery of the true owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e0a802a151552f677dd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.31", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God fixed a day to judge the inhabited world", "note": "Paul's Areopagus speech gives the judgment day a universal scope, closely corresponding to the Lord of the worlds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a590c2f3771eab97107e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses is adopted as Pharaoh's daughter’s son", "note": "Stephen retells the adoption by Pharaoh's daughter, confirming the Torah's detail while the Qur'anic retelling shifts Pharaoh himself into the speaker who claims to have raised Moses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d95f57b3d8caf5db8c1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Pharaoh's daughter takes Moses and raises him", "note": "Stephen retells the royal adoption in the same terms that frame the commentary's Pharaoh-and-Moses dispute."}
{"connection_id": "BC-22c0eb86b1a680597c4e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.22", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses learns Egyptian wisdom", "note": "Stephen says Moses was educated in Egyptian wisdom and became powerful in words and deeds, a concrete counterpart to the commentary's claim that the true Lord gives Moses judgment and messenger authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4f7db4f1fae175afc7f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses turns toward his oppressed kin", "note": "At forty Moses visits his Israelite brothers, matching the commentary's movement from palace upbringing to the enslaved people he identifies with."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85b8f866335faf9547dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses sees an Israelite being wronged", "note": "Moses defends the oppressed man, giving the royal-raised Moses' first decisive action for his enslaved people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-713a13ca5ca0592f0f0a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses thought his brothers would understand his deliverance", "note": "Stephen supplies a positive intention for Moses's intervention among the oppressed, an interpretive counterpoint to the commentary's self-description as among the lost."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4687f2032cd7bb90dd38", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.29", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses escapes to Midian", "note": "Stephen says Moses fled and became a resident alien in Midian, matching the commentary's move from Pharaoh's palace to the well journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c1f675f2269b00e51b1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.34", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God sees the people's oppression and sends Moses", "note": "The Lord says he has seen Israel's suffering and sends Moses to deliver them, matching the commentary's contrast between Pharaoh's boast and the real Lord's action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7a2fa2ac0ebefe4e8a5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.35", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The rejected Moses becomes ruler and deliverer", "note": "Stephen says God sent as ruler and deliverer the very Moses whom Israel rejected, a precise reversal of Pharaoh's claimed benefaction and a strong instance of the commentary's true-Rab contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6116322e7429a710e34", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.9.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the community called the Way", "note": "Acts turns a communal allegiance into the name of a way, a specific but non-narrative counterpart to the commentary's path joined to a people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6800286092579f1b990a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.2.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The written debt record is erased", "note": "Paul says the record of debt against people is canceled and removed, a Christian theological reading that turns the commentary's written account into an image of forgiven guilt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26fb16d1efbe2c55ef10", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.2.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "record of debt with legal demands erased", "note": "Paul's cancelled written debt gives a precise Christian analogue to the commentary's written obligation and its final settlement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-509bdd4a793326327852", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "new and living way opened through the curtain", "note": "The sanctuary is entered by a named living way, a precise Christian meeting of route and the divine house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-897cb0645139602dc8ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses refuses the royal identity", "note": "Moses refuses to be called Pharaoh's daughter's son, directly meeting the commentary's contrast between palace upbringing and voluntary belonging to God's people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-285a2f6ed738e00f35d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses refuses to be called Pharaoh's daughter's son", "note": "Hebrews interprets Moses's adult choice as rejection of the royal identity, reinforcing the commentary's contrast of imposed palace status and chosen allegiance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f80bbb5b68a09c73bf6e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "He chooses hardship with God's people", "note": "Moses chooses mistreatment with the people of God over temporary pleasure, a specific counterpart to the commentary's enslaved people and willing servant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a550b9d99319ea39444", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.26", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "He values reproach above Egypt's wealth", "note": "Moses regards disgrace for God's anointed one as greater wealth than Egypt's treasure, clarifying why royal nurture does not define his allegiance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72555b2ed95860a97775", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make straight paths for your feet; the lame be healed", "note": "The command retains both the physical track made by feet and the contrast of injury with restored upright movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b21f50edce7c23a8a2b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sacrifice of praise and lips confessing God's name", "note": "The verse makes praise of the divine name a sacrifice, closely joining the commentary's name, praise, and ritual offering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-169d7fa799e4c5c88e49", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God brings up the great shepherd of the sheep", "note": "Hebrews names the eschatological shepherd in a covenant setting, directly meeting herd, sacrifice, and true guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98d65491598600ea2a54", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God equips people to do God's will", "note": "The prayer for divine equipping gives a Christian form to the commentary's servant who asks for help in willing obedience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b277cf279d409da8c549", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "they always go astray and have not known my ways", "note": "Hebrews carries the Psalm's language of straying and unknown ways into a Christian warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b0609a15d52ee557a80", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "they shall not enter my rest in my wrath", "note": "The next verse retains wrath and denied rest, sharpening the commentary's opposed destinations."}

