{"connection_id": "BC-057dd4c57f3501a2e63e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The nations are separated like a flock", "note": "The judge separates people as a shepherd separates sheep from goats, matching the commentary's meeting of judgment, herd, and divided outcomes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0491f4a5da9ec9fe3967", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all nations gathered and separated as sheep from goats", "note": "The final judgment is staged as a shepherd's sorting of animals, directly meeting herd imagery and the day of account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7214611f11faae8b112", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.33", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Sheep on the right, goats on the left", "note": "The placement of two groups on opposite sides gives a concrete parallel to the commentary's blessed, wrathful, and lost groups."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40cdb06a94b082871bcf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.33", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sheep on the right and goats on the left", "note": "The bodily arrangement of the flock makes the judgment's two outcomes spatial, complementing the commentary's two ways of being lost."}
{"connection_id": "BC-88a6c1e0a80c62c7b18c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.34", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The king blesses and invites inheritance", "note": "The king calls the blessed to inherit what was prepared for them, joining divine kingship, favor, and final recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d70a502fa45ea3202244", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.35", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the king receives food and drink given to the needy", "note": "In the judgment scene, service to the hungry and thirsty is received by the king himself, linking need, willing service, and din."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc438cd7043206e0805c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.40", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Mercy toward the needy is service to the king", "note": "The king identifies care for the least with care for himself, giving a specific ethical meaning to the commentary's freely offered service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f05baf617099ad67ac8d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The other group departs under a curse", "note": "The condemned are sent away from the king, a counter-destination to the blessed path and welcome described in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ea56eae77cfbc1b0c73", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cursed depart into eternal fire", "note": "The king's adverse verdict supplies an explicit Christian judgement-and-wrath counterpart to the angry path's end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c23fe85391dfa89f1346", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Two final destinations", "note": "The passage ends with punishment for one group and life for the other, a direct counterpart to the commentary's divided outcomes on the judgment day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ddf621df2d386af6b221", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.4.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Worship and serve God alone", "note": "Jesus refuses Satan's offer by saying worship belongs to God and service to God alone; this gives the commentary's freely chosen service a precise scriptural formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c4163d506cc42dc1b2f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Kingdom and divine will", "note": "The prayer asks that God's kingdom come and his will be done, placing divine rule before the ensuing human petitions as the Fatiha places praise and lordship before its request for guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0574e6bacea7bd94f4fd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Daily provision", "note": "The petition for daily bread gives a concrete counterpart to the commentary's picture of the needy traveler asking the Lord for help and provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7661a16623af38680ddd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "give us today our daily bread", "note": "The shared prayer moves from address to requested provision, matching the commentary's needy traveller and nurturing Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50963baff97609d41be6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Debt and forgiveness", "note": "The prayer asks God to forgive debts as worshippers forgive debtors, bringing the commentary's day of repayment and its debt imagery into a prayer of dependence on mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b9e109a4503e11e4760", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgive us our debts", "note": "The petition uses debt to name what requires divine resolution, directly accompanying the commentary's din-and-borrowing imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2e0824c522141a34008", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Guidance away from trial and evil", "note": "The prayer asks not to be led into trial and to be rescued from evil, a specific negative counterpart to the Fatiha's request to be led on the straight path and kept from its failed routes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7b500287399b1e0df04", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not lead us into trial; deliver us from evil", "note": "The final petition asks not to be led into a destructive course and for rescue, closely paralleling help and the requested right way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7188f6636523b7378277", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no one can serve two masters", "note": "Jesus's master-servant saying supplies a focused Christian bridge between worship, ownership, and the commentary's contrast of true and false masters."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b1b43f8dd9202e54c02", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prayer opens with God's name", "note": "The Lord's Prayer addresses the heavenly Father and asks that his name be hallowed, a close liturgical meeting with the Fatiha's opening invocation of God's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2601efefa68ae9cf5fd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "our Father; hallowed be your name", "note": "The Lord's Prayer opens with a named, direct address to God, closely accompanying the commentary's movement from divine name to second-person devotion."}

