{"connection_id": "BC-779e9f23ad997fd13589", "ref": "WLC:Deut.28.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Refusing to heed brings a curse", "note": "The counterpart covenant warning makes disobedience lead to a curse, a specific relation between a community's conduct and the commentary's images of favor and wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a524794ce4d09e819bd", "ref": "WLC:Deut.28.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Blessing accompanies the community's way", "note": "The covenant blessing reaches the people in their city and field, giving a concrete communal form to the Fatiha's request for the path of those favored."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72ff7083af03f804fce9", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.35", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "recompense, a slipping foot, and the near day of calamity", "note": "The Song of Moses connects repayment and an imminent day with a foot that slips, closely matching debt, judgment, and falling from the path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-102af5e2aeb88711f6e8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.4.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Luminaries are not to be worshipped", "note": "Deuteronomy names sun, moon, and stars as created lights allotted to peoples and forbids worshipping them, matching the commentary's distinction between a sign and what it signifies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffc411cbf292a52f83d3", "ref": "WLC:Deut.4.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sun, moon, and stars must not be bowed to or served", "note": "The warning against serving the host of heaven is a direct biblical counterpart to the commentary's sun-worshippers who mistake sign for Rabb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-35afe392c5ace4307c38", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words bound as a sign on hand and forehead", "note": "The covenantal sign worn on the body gives a Jewish scriptural counterpart to the commentary's image of name and stamp."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34b5d1d2a05c9ff659ce", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God leads through dry wilderness and brings water from rock", "note": "Deuteronomy explicitly binds wilderness leading to water for a thirsty people, closely matching the commentary's guided journey and well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ee2ed96324f07f2d5ea", "ref": "WLC:Exod.1.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Egyptians ruthlessly made Israel serve", "note": "Exodus supplies the forced enslavement that the commentary sets against Moses's willing service to the real Rabb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85f6698380ab21276608", "ref": "WLC:Exod.12.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lamb taken according to each household", "note": "The Passover instruction joins a selected flock-animal to a household, offering a concrete biblical counterpart to sacrifice brought home."}
{"connection_id": "BC-83104c91bc481f2df880", "ref": "WLC:Exod.13.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God goes before the travelers", "note": "YHWH leads Israel by cloud and fire on the way, a specific counterpart to the guide walking ahead of a traveler and the leader of a herd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b46269172215f62dad3", "ref": "WLC:Exod.13.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "YHWH goes before to lead them by the way", "note": "The pillar goes before Israel specifically in order to lead them on the journey, a direct analogue for the guide preceding the traveller."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7fd77d33e522d9cc818c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses brings water from the rock for the thirsty people", "note": "The Exodus water scene gives Moses a second, communal road-and-water episode beside the Midian well developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-764c1d85e83431a42898", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses is raised in Pharaoh's house", "note": "Pharaoh's daughter takes Moses as her son after rescuing him, the narrative behind the later claim of royal rearing that the commentary sets against the true Lord's gift."}
