{"connection_id": "BC-0db902bf04bfd55aa07d", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The day is wrath, distress, and devastation", "note": "Zephaniah calls YHWH's day a day of wrath and distress, giving the commentary's judgment day a sharply adverse side."}
{"connection_id": "BC-22f15dd2e4d1b994647c", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The day brings alarm against fortified cities", "note": "The trumpet and alarm on that day give a specific public scene of judgment rather than a generic reference to divine anger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bf6b00b653deeed0c61", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.18", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The day consumes the earth in divine anger", "note": "The oracle says neither silver nor gold can rescue people from YHWH's wrath, adding a concrete reckoning to the commentary's account of judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48ae31d334049f596294", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day God rises to gather nations and pour wrath", "note": "Zephaniah combines divine rising, gathered peoples, and poured wrath in a final-day scene beside the commentary's standing and anger imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3efab4f6f0996753f0ca", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 10:16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wisdom enters the servant of the Lord against dread kings", "note": "Wisdom portrays Moses as God's servant confronting royal power, giving a late-Jewish bridge between the commentary's true Rabb and Pharaoh's coercion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9aacd07b3df9e20ed326", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all things arranged by measure, number, and weight", "note": "Wisdom makes cosmic order a matter of measure and weight, directly enlarging the commentary's scale from commerce to the worlds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aeb9be1444ecbafd6792", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "People fail to recognize the creator", "note": "Wisdom says people who observe the world did not recognize its maker, the same error the commentary assigns to worshippers who take a rising sign for the Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-00d75e310193713194f7", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "They treat created lights as gods", "note": "The text names fire, wind, stars, and lights of heaven as things mistakenly taken for gods, closely matching the commentary's star, moon, and sun scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f5ec765c8436434adb4", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sun, moon, stars, and heavenly lights treated as gods", "note": "Wisdom lists the celestial bodies people mistake for rulers of the world, an unusually close Jewish parallel to the commentary's Abrahamic scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6f22c5da69e8df3cb262", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Creation's greatness points to its maker", "note": "Wisdom argues that the beauty and power of created things should lead observers to their maker, exactly the sign-versus-signified distinction in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-998fe616af76d0705f0c", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "creator perceived from the greatness of created things", "note": "The argument from visible beauty to the Maker directly articulates the commentary's rule that a sign is not the thing it signals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3dbc2f735c5d372a8941", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Searching creation should lead to its Lord", "note": "The people investigate created things but are captivated by their appearance; this explains the commentary's claim that the worshippers stop at the sign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-323603a758e4dbacae71", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Ignorance is not excused", "note": "Wisdom says such people remain blameworthy, giving moral force to the commentary's contrast between a sign and worship of the created object."}
{"connection_id": "BC-22135e1321a817171063", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 13:9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "They should have found the Lord sooner", "note": "Those who can investigate the world should have been able to find its Lord, closely matching the commentary's lostness when a sign is mistaken for its owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d98c05a42e6029c67ab8", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous stand with great confidence", "note": "The righteous standing before their former oppressors closely matches the commentary's upright figure set against those who fall under wrath."}

