{"connection_id": "BC-b5e5ade5caa60e9109ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The guide brings his followers to the house", "note": "Jesus promises to return and take his followers to himself, connecting a trusted guide with arrival at the Father's house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1755941aa93f3764fb51", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The disciples are shown the way to the destination", "note": "Jesus says the disciples know the way to where he is going, a direct meeting of guide, road, and destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-589f36d87c5bf6f496f9", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus identifies himself as the way to the Father", "note": "Jesus calls himself the way and says access to the Father is through him, a Christian interpretation that makes the requested path personal and relational."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0dff3e3ac501403b7c98", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "I am the way to the Father", "note": "The way is identified with the mediator who brings people to the Father, a direct Christian meeting of route, guide, and home."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48ee414a25be4425510d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.3.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ʿālamīn; Syriac ʿālmā", "note": "The source's “worlds” and the Syriac cognate ʿālmā meet in the Peshitta's statement that God loved the world, linking divine lordship to the world as the object of God's care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f548376a87de631267ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "living water given at the well", "note": "At the traveller's well, water becomes a divine gift; this extends the commentary's physical water-and-need scene into John's symbolic register."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a16e7ce15208d3683b72", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becomes a spring to eternal life", "note": "John turns water received at the well into a spring within the receiver, a distinct continuation of the commentary's water-and-need image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6b7460875f1672a9969", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus, wearied by travel, sits at Jacob's well", "note": "The scene makes a tired traveller, a road, and a well coincide, closely matching the commentary's Moses cluster."}
{"connection_id": "BC-152fdcc300bc8b4ec00d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.37", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the thirsty are invited to come and drink", "note": "Jesus's public invitation makes thirst a route toward divinely given water, closely accompanying the commentary's needy traveller."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7f8e20c92a5a53f9286", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kept from stumbling and made to stand", "note": "Jude pairs divine preservation from falling with standing before glory, exactly reversing the commentary's falling under wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e950f13240be4ac9045d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.78", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's mercy brings dawn to those in darkness", "note": "Zechariah's song says God's tender mercy brings light to those sitting in darkness, joining mercy with the commentary's lostness and its contrast between radiant and dark outcomes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-176e22cfb9b41d743262", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.79", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God guides feet into the way of peace", "note": "The song says the dawn will guide people's feet into the way of peace, closely matching the Fatiha's request for a straight path and the commentary's walking image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c38e820d737d8810c12a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.79", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide our feet into the way of peace", "note": "Zechariah's hymn gives the guide the precise task of directing feet into a saving way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-422840541f0aa646666a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prayer begins with Father and holy name", "note": "Jesus' prayer addresses the Father and asks that his name be hallowed, adding Luke's distinct liturgical form to the Fatiha's named invocation and direct address."}
{"connection_id": "BC-63a0da56e1b8a89cc1c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Daily bread as dependence", "note": "The prayer asks for daily bread, giving a concrete petition for provision within the same short prayer that asks for God's reign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d1c77c7dd847e91fdcc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Forgiveness and protection from misdirection", "note": "The prayer asks forgiveness and not to be brought into trial, a distinct compact counterpart to the Fatiha's dependence, guidance, and avoidance of failed paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-915ee3a21586bdc210ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.58", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "settle with an accuser while on the way to the magistrate", "note": "Luke makes the road itself the place to resolve a debt before judgment, an unusually close meeting of path, debt, and day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4394cad5dc47113bad50", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The owner searches for one lost sheep", "note": "The shepherd leaves the ninety-nine to seek the lost one, making the lost animal's relation to its owner the center of Jesus' parable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e9938347028fbd7901b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seek the one lost sheep until he finds it", "note": "The search for the strayed sheep gives the commentary's lost animal a returning shepherd and a deliberate homeward movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba76077aeb3c064b40c1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The lost sheep is carried home", "note": "The shepherd puts the found sheep on his shoulders, a concrete rescue and return that reverses the commentary's animal wandering without a known owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7cf33175b4a0e1f49f16", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The recovery becomes shared joy", "note": "The shepherd calls friends to rejoice over the found sheep, giving the lost animal a known owner and restored community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2172c4f0955c8dca0294", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joy over repentance", "note": "Jesus interprets the sheep as a sinner who repents, making the recovery a moral return to God rather than a mere animal story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4e5b620f076ae1acd8c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Debtors called to account", "note": "The steward summons debtors and asks the amount owed, a direct scene of accounts being settled that clarifies the commentary's debt imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-415ec2864afa02e93df1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "A debt is reduced in writing", "note": "The manager tells a debtor to alter the written amount, turning the commentary's upright, witnessed debt record into a deliberate manipulation within Jesus' parable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af3d72b9f34c1ad69192", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Another debt is rewritten", "note": "A second written obligation is reduced as well; the parable's manager uses debt documents for advantage, a concrete counter-scene to the commentary's just record and balance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7077a0d6ed7f9d1c5edb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Two people go to the temple to pray", "note": "Jesus frames the contrast as a temple encounter, giving a concrete communal setting to the commentary's worshipper speaking directly to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-303fe6cb25a9cb2f7b6a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The tax collector asks God for mercy without lifting his eyes", "note": "The petitioner stands at a distance and asks for mercy, a specific posture of dependence that echoes the commentary's needy servant asking the Lord for help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09303c9e8786ab50114c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The humble petitioner goes home justified", "note": "Jesus says the humble petitioner, rather than the self-exalting one, is justified; this joins prayer, moral standing, and the final verdict."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5fa6507bf9166f7993e4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.21.28", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stand upright and lift your heads at redemption", "note": "Jesus's command gives the positive posture opposed to the commentary's face-down falling under wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-701360d3727d5bd8fd09", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.1.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Mark applies Isaiah's road to John's preparation", "note": "Mark identifies Isaiah's wilderness voice with John the Baptist preparing the Lord's way; this Christian reading makes the straight road a call to prepare for God's arrival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c81352454eace9cfd71d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.6.34", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sheep without a shepherd", "note": "Jesus reads an unguided crowd through the exact herd-and-leader image the commentary develops for hadi."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54f9e2ca64f0732bb62b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.28", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The weary are invited to come for rest", "note": "Jesus invites the weary and burdened to come to him for rest, matching the commentary's tired traveler who asks for help."}

