{"connection_id": "BC-cfee37ed6ffbab7f85f7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.29", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A gentle teacher gives a yoke", "note": "Jesus calls disciples to take his yoke and learn from him because he is gentle and humble; as a discipleship metaphor, it connects the commentary's voluntarily tamed servant with rest under a trusted guide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95592a2f6b92cad3299b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.29", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "take my yoke; learn from me; find rest", "note": "Jesus turns the animal yoke into willing discipleship and rest, directly meeting the commentary's tamed servant and weary traveller."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be756c93f516a1023bf0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The teacher's yoke is easy", "note": "Jesus says his yoke is easy and burden light, giving a concrete contrast to coercive enslavement in the commentary's Moses scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc62f9ec1365e1469b35", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "my yoke is easy and my burden light", "note": "The easy yoke and light burden give a concrete Christian counterpart to submissive service that does not crush the one who seeks help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-31c3837a191966ceded6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.36", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "give account on the day of judgment", "note": "Jesus explicitly uses the language of rendering an account on the final day, closely meeting the commentary's debt-and-record imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-646ac0ef74f4f34d9887", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.43", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous shine like the sun in their Father's kingdom", "note": "The final shining of the righteous gives a Christian counterpart to the commentary's contrasting judgment-day faces."}
{"connection_id": "BC-072da806495436356863", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A shepherd seeks the strayed one", "note": "Jesus asks whether a shepherd would not seek a sheep that strayed, echoing the commentary's last image of an animal lost from its owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b105bbd842b5d876b57", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joy when the strayed sheep is found", "note": "The shepherd rejoices over the recovered sheep, emphasizing the owner's active care that contrasts with the commentary's unrecognized master."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4896d1a441f8c29fe07", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A king settles accounts with his servants", "note": "Jesus begins a parable with a king who calls servants to settle their debts, bringing the commentary's king, day of repayment, and debt together in one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd2deab638d07340fcb6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a king settles accounts with his servants", "note": "The parable puts king, servants, debt, and reckoning in one scene, directly paralleling Malik, abd, and the day debt is closed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8174f514b7aa5b0a4022", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A servant owes an unpayable sum", "note": "The first servant's vast debt makes the account concrete and sets up the king's decision about repayment and mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cad4db8bfbb8faac912b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.26", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The debtor asks for patience", "note": "The servant falls down and asks the king for patience, matching the commentary's dependent petitioner before a sovereign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdedce48379c3ae37763", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.27", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The king releases the debt out of compassion", "note": "The king has compassion and cancels the debt, combining sovereignty, repayment, and mercy in a direct parallel to the commentary's divine attributes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8739042d74ebd860ac54", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.28", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The forgiven servant demands payment from another", "note": "The servant then seizes a fellow servant over a smaller debt, turning received mercy into a test of how the forgiven person treats others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70b55dc0c84360a23869", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.32", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The king recalls the canceled debt", "note": "The king confronts the servant with the debt he had canceled, making remembered mercy and account-keeping central to judgment."}
