{"connection_id": "BC-0542f567a03ea9eb1f47", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The broad way ends in destruction", "note": "Jesus contrasts a broad road and gate leading to destruction with the narrow entrance, giving a concrete two-way choice for the commentary's path and its failures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a831eacb8c4e3f9277e0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The narrow way leads to life", "note": "The difficult road leads to life and is found by few, joining path, guidance, and destination in Jesus' own teaching."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47383daa0800bf187ff5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow way leading to life", "note": "The contrast of a life-giving way with another route gives an explicit Christian form to the commentary's road with two divergent endings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f872b0ed01b5530d080", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the measure used is measured back", "note": "The same measure becomes judicial reciprocity, a compact Christian counterpart to balance and account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2fd6bbaa1461df62e4f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fear God, give glory, judgment hour, worship the creator", "note": "This angelic proclamation joins praise, judgment, worship, creation, and springs of water in one unusually close parallel to the commentary's whole movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92954e8768139831c54e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.19.1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hallelujah: salvation, glory, and power belong to God", "note": "The heavenly acclamation places praise before the declaration of judgment that follows, paralleling the commentary's hamd before the narrated gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a2c211889a384b726ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.19.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's judgments are true and just", "note": "Revelation answers the praise with true and just judgment, directly joining hamd, din, and the correction of corrupt judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6ffabab3b5144e185149", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dead stand, books open, judged by recorded deeds", "note": "Revelation combines standing persons, opened books, and individual judgment, the central visual cluster of the commentary's day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-310b8d4fa58f3fc07f37", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the thirsty may take the water of life freely", "note": "The final invitation joins thirst, gift, and the completed eschatological home developed at the commentary's road's end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ded681b823d42c381030", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's servants worship before the throne", "note": "The final city presents uncoerced service before the true ruler, a culminating Christian counterpart to na'budu."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdcaf8e4e5543de35943", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "they see his face and bear his name", "note": "The named servants see God's face in the final home, reversing the commentary's faceless loss, wrath, and unknown ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e64c3fecc45bd127ee48", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.6.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hide from the face of the enthroned one and the Lamb's wrath", "note": "The terrified call for mountains to fall over them gives an exact Christian image of wrath, faces, and falling."}
{"connection_id": "BC-272a9023f4f8e3043e41", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.6.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the great day of wrath has come; who can stand", "note": "The verse explicitly sets wrath's day against the ability to stand, matching the commentary's fall-versus-upright contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b4698dbbf6c0bbeaa0d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.6.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "black horse holds a balance scale", "note": "The apocalyptic balance makes measure part of a judgment sequence, extending the commentary's mizan into a Christian final-day image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6003109dde116c81e158", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Lamb shepherds to living water", "note": "The Lamb leads the multitude to springs of living water and God wipes away tears, uniting the commentary's herd, guide, water, and final relief in an eschatological scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-718b312b0d2a32fc06be", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lamb shepherds and guides to springs", "note": "The Lamb who is also shepherd fuses sacrificial animal, herd leader, and water-guidance in an eschatological form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-51cdb6c4a1ef010cf626", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seal God's servants on their foreheads before harm", "note": "The seal marks divine servants before approaching catastrophe, closely corresponding to the commentary's stamp, ownership, and wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4e764f8a9ad12ab9374", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.1.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Knowing God but failing to honor him", "note": "Paul says people knew God yet did not glorify or thank him, matching the commentary's astral worshippers who encounter signs but fail to direct praise to their source."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77a25ebf7db1b636b177", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.1.22", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Claimed wisdom becomes foolishness", "note": "Paul says those who claim wisdom became fools, a pointed counterpart to the commentary's people who misread the heavens and lose the way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d286b3f72f5e79f4ce0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.1.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The creator's glory is exchanged for images", "note": "Paul describes exchanging God's glory for images of created beings, directly matching the commentary's distinction between a sign and the divine reality it points toward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2f641d4f03ebd4e9c22", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.1.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The creature is worshipped instead of the creator", "note": "Paul's explicit contrast between creator and creature matches the commentary's claim that sun-worshippers confuse the sign with the one signified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7f7e6352c99c1dbec46", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.1.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "served the creature rather than the Creator", "note": "Paul makes the inversion of worship explicit, directly matching the commentary's warning against taking a created heavenly sign for its Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba4f33ad6ee40ca1b439", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "present bodies as a living sacrifice in worship", "note": "Paul turns voluntary embodied worship into sacrifice, a direct Christian counterpart to the commentary's chosen servant and offered animal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-200e416375fbfe0be38f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each gives an account of himself to God", "note": "The individual account to God directly maps the commentary's day when every person stands before the Lord of worlds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73002bf57d2cb782d534", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day of wrath and righteous judgment", "note": "Paul's phrase directly joins wrath to an appointed judicial day, the pair the commentary gathers around din."}
{"connection_id": "BC-416f9a2d6f1381445bd6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.6.22", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "freed from sin and enslaved to God", "note": "Paul's paradox of liberation into God's service gives a sharp Christian analogue to voluntary servitude under the true owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-732124a81bdb10c3993b", "ref": "1QS 4:2-14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ways of truth and perversity; peace or wrath", "note": "The Rule of the Community develops two opposed ways whose outcomes are peace or divine wrath, supplying a precise Second Temple counterpart to the two faces of losing the path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce7296d5e9e6fca76283", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:3-9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow path to a city between fire and water", "note": "The vision's perilous single route toward an inheritable city makes road, water, danger, and destination a striking Second Temple counterpart to the commentary's way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f901aeaeca472b2a6fdb", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Rosh Hashanah 16b", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "three books opened at the new-year judgment", "note": "The Talmud's books for righteous, wicked, and intermediate persons make the judgment day an explicit written accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2f269902ebd42d71119", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Rosh Hashanah 18a", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "benei maron read through sheep or a narrow pass", "note": "The Talmud's interpretations of the Mishnah's procession make individual passage before the judge resemble either a flock or a constricted route, joining herd, path, and judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9aa529825ff63fb1773c", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 31a", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "first judgment question asks whether business was conducted faithfully", "note": "The Talmud makes honest commercial dealing the first question at judgment, a precise rabbinic bridge between false measure and final account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8e13c22d49834a2eb8a", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud Sotah 12b", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses refuses Egyptian nurses and is nursed by his mother", "note": "The tradition makes Pharaoh's household dependent on Moses's own mother for his nurture, intensifying the commentary's challenge to Pharaoh's claim of upbringing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c14568ebed9126399ac8", "ref": "Baruch 5:7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "level ground so Israel may walk safely", "note": "Baruch makes the leveling of mountains and valleys serve Israel's safe walking, a close late-Jewish parallel to the straight road's communal movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da3757bbdc69fc507d71", "ref": "Baruch 5:8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trees shade Israel at God's command", "note": "The commanded shade gives a direct textual companion to the commentary's road, water, and shade cluster."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13833f776892fc0f302a", "ref": "Baruch 5:9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God leads Israel with mercy and righteousness", "note": "The closing promise combines divine leading, a people on the move, mercy, and righteousness in the terms developed around rahim and guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f9a91a4b6032aebf638", "ref": "Exodus Rabbah 2:2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses is tested as a shepherd", "note": "The midrash says Moses' care for a lamb at the well proves his fitness to shepherd Israel; it turns the Midian watering episode into the qualification of the future guide of a people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fee10805115538cb0d76", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 38:13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham's anti-idol argument in Terah's workshop", "note": "The midrash recasts Abraham's discernment of false gods as a contest with made images, extending the commentary's sign-versus-Lord distinction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8315ada3cafe3ad306c1", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 39:1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "The world is a palace with an owner", "note": "The midrash pictures Abraham seeing a palace on fire and asking whether it can lack an owner; God reveals himself as the master. It interprets creation as a sign that directs the seeker to its owner, close to the commentary's night-sky lesson."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a858b133733dab718002", "ref": "Josephus, Antiquities 1.154", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham teaches one creator rather than celestial powers", "note": "Josephus interprets Abraham as arguing from the ordered cosmos to one creator, a close historical reception of the commentary's astral scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57942467326c94a2916b", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "serve not for the sake of reward", "note": "Avot distinguishes service for reward from service freely given to the master, clarifying the commentary's contrast between coerced slavery and chosen worship."}

