{"connection_id": "BC-b1b4eeefd028c0841cb8", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses flees Pharaoh to Midian", "note": "The threatened Moses escapes Pharaoh and reaches Midian, matching the commentary's transition from royal upbringing and forced power to the Midian journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01248524ceb41f3676a2", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses flees to Midian and sits by a well", "note": "The verse fixes the journey and the well that the commentary gathers into Moses's guided road-and-water scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd0771ad669b96aa538e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A well and shepherding daughters", "note": "Jethro's daughters come to draw water for their father's flock, placing the well, water, and herd at the center of Moses' Midian scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4ecb353cbface6e68a9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Midian's daughters draw water and fill troughs", "note": "The source scene explicitly joins the well, women drawing water, and troughs for a flock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6935da5657dfda5a08d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses helps the women water the flock", "note": "Moses stands up for the women and waters their flock, closely matching the commentary's Moses helping others at the well before resting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-edc510ead3d448a428ea", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses helps the women and waters their flock", "note": "Moses's intervention at the well culminates in watering another's flock, the direct biblical parallel to the commentary's scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-994e149199733756e2e4", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.18", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The women return to their father", "note": "The daughters report their encounter to Reuel, carrying the well scene into a household that receives Moses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d8cbb211bcd7a26812d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.18", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the women return to their father's house", "note": "The return home after the well episode adds the household destination that the commentary gives to the road and sacrificial herd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b3b5f197217308ffe49", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses is remembered as a rescuer and water-drawer", "note": "The women describe an Egyptian who rescued them and drew water, matching the commentary's specific action of helping at the well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47de13f6ade61660b977", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Hospitality follows the well encounter", "note": "Reuel asks why the daughters left the man behind and invites him to eat, linking the journey and water scene to welcome at a house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d58d004e6f448ff5575", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses stays in Midian", "note": "Moses consents to dwell with Reuel, an arrival and household destination after the road and well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1a91fd13d3c9c04d43b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Moses's mother nurses him for Pharaoh's daughter", "note": "Where the Qur'anic Pharaoh presents upbringing as a claim upon Moses, Exodus makes his own mother the nurse and Pharaoh's daughter the payer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de2765ac4b5b74501842", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "an angel guards the way and brings Israel to a prepared place", "note": "The messenger goes before Israel to protect the road and bring them to a destination, giving a close biblical form to the helper-guide and final home."}
{"connection_id": "BC-83bfa1b09cbd8b18aad5", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God sends Moses back to Pharaoh", "note": "The God who sees Israel's oppression sends Moses to bring the people out, matching the commentary's contrast between Pharaoh's coercive claim and the true Lord's commission."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5168a0fd2b1aa85a8f64", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Freedom's purpose is service to God", "note": "God promises that after the exodus the people will serve him at this mountain, a direct answer to the commentary's contrast between forced slavery and freely chosen worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34bb8a2c92b876aba5a0", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "my name forever; my memorial for generations", "note": "God's self-naming gives a scriptural counterpart to the commentary's beginning with a name that identifies the owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4536809e5193cc78bf6a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Merciful and gracious divine name", "note": "YHWH proclaims himself compassionate, gracious, and abundant in steadfast love; the paired mercy language is a close scriptural counterpart to the Fatiha's repeated merciful names."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6fe30d18ba81cb3b6b0e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "rahum, mercy, and slow anger", "note": "YHWH proclaims himself compassionate and slow to anger; Hebrew rahum shares the r-h-m root with the commentary's rahim and directly sets mercy beside wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-570a3d09bb286b6165b9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Mercy that forgives wrongdoing", "note": "The continuation specifies forgiveness alongside accountability for wrongdoing, joining the commentary's mercy with its account of judgment and repayment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4605060c9184adc7980e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.22", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Israel is God's firstborn son", "note": "Calling enslaved Israel God's son places divine parenthood over Pharaoh's claimed control, sharpening the false-nurturer and true-Rabb contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aecf6f246b512a42dd9e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The enslaved son is released to serve YHWH", "note": "Moses must tell Pharaoh to let God's son go so that he may serve God, joining coerced enslavement, divine parenthood, and worship in one claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b701608719501931ea5", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "YHWH himself seeks his sheep", "note": "God says he will search for and seek his sheep, directly answering the commentary's final image of an animal separated from its owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8530d83f3aed0776ac2", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The scattered sheep are rescued", "note": "YHWH rescues his flock from the places where it was scattered, giving a specific counterpart to the lost animal whose trace has vanished."}
{"connection_id": "BC-99cba6cf1cf2cb54a55f", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gathered flock brought to its land and fed", "note": "Ezekiel's shepherd brings scattered sheep home and feeds them on their own land, supplying the return-to-home side of the commentary's herd route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2673fb068f118b5a2174", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd makes the flock lie down", "note": "YHWH says he will feed his sheep and make them lie down, bringing herd, nourishment, and rest together."}

